+### [SDF Volume Rendering](19065-sdf-volume-rendering.md)
+Integrates color and density along rays sampled from an implicit SDF. It converts SDF values to density using a Laplace distribution CDF and aggregates color using standard volume rendering weights. Unlike standard volume rendering which uses raw density fields, this method leverages the geometric priors of the SDF to better recover underlying geometry. Choose when recovering geometry from implicit fields is the primary goal.
 
 ### FBM-based Implicit Density Definition
-Defines an implicit volumetric object where density $\rho$ is derived from an FBM field $f$. Density is zero when $f(\mathbf{p}) < v$, and remapped via function $h$ when $f(\mathbf{p}) \ge v$. The 0-isosurface of $\rho$ corresponds to the v-isosurface of $f$ [S8].
+Defines an implicit volumetric object where density $\rho$ is derived from an FBM field $f$. Density is zero when $f(\mathbf{p}) < v$, and remapped via function $h$ when $f(\mathbf{p}) \ge v$. The 0-isosurface of $\rho$ corresponds to the v-isosurface of $f$ [S9].
 Formula:
 $$ \rho(\mathbf{p}) = \begin{cases} h(f(\mathbf{p})) & \text{when } f(\mathbf{p}) \geq v \\ 0 & \text{otherwise} \end{cases} $$
-where $f(\mathbf{p}) = B_n(\mathbf{p})$ is the FBM field, $v$ is the threshold (0 until Section 7), and $h$ is a positive remapping function [S8].
-Pitfall: If $h$ is not a positive function, the 0-isosurface of $\rho$ will not correspond to the v-isosurface of $f$. The method assumes $h$ is positive [S8].
